{"execution_score":6,"aesthetic_score":5,"feedback":"The Rousseau idea lands partially — flat foliage quads in discrete depth layers with a restrained green palette is the right instinct, and the rare yellow bloom accents are a nice touch. But the Lucy statue as centerpiece fights the brief: Rousseau painted jungles with animals and figures embedded in foliage, not classical sculptures. The foliage reads more as confetti rectangles than Rousseau's dense, overlapping, meticulously outlined leaves — try elongated rounded shapes with visible vein lines or layered opacity to get that flat-but-lush tapestry feel. The camera is static and distant; pulling in tighter and letting the leaves occlude the figure more (80% hidden, eyes peeking through) would channel Rousseau's dreamlike fixed-gaze compositions far better than a fully visible statue with sparse leaf scatter."}
